          "jcr:lastModifiedBy": "user-c8b824f5b1@example.invalid",
          "jcr:created": "Thu Mar 17 2022 18:41:35 GMT+0000",
          "text": "<h2>Avoiding damage to transvenous leads - a comparison of electrocautery techniques and two insulated electrocautery blades<br>\n</h2>\n<p><b>Authors:</b>&nbsp;Jeremiah Wasserlauf, MD, MS, Taiki Esheim BS, MBA, Natasha M. Jarett BS, Eric K.Y. Chan, PhD, Robert D. Schaller, DO, Fermin C. Garcia, MD, Bradley P. Knight, MD</p>\n<p>Electrocautery (cautery) can damage transvenous cardiac device leads. The purpose of this study was to compare lead damage from an insulated cautery blade when used with several different techniques that included coagulation (COAG) versus cutting (CUT) mode, perpendicular active edge (active) versus parallel flat blade (flat) orientation (phase 1), and using one commercially available blade versus another (phase 1).<br>\n</p>\n",
          "jcr:lastModified": "Fri Apr 22 2022 21:33:32 GMT+0000",
          "sling:resourceType": "stryker/components/content/text",
          "textIsRich": "true"
        },
